    "body": "Environmental groups warn seabed mining may harm fragile marine habitats and disrupt ecosystems. ( Stock image by magdal3na. )\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 19, 2026\nThe first United States auction of deep-sea mineral leases remained on the federal schedule for noon Eastern time Thursday after a judge threw out the fisheries agency’s sign-off for the sale off American Samoa. The Marine Minerals Administration is offering two tracts covering 31.5 million acres of Pacific seabed under leases with an initial term of 20 years. Bidding is to open at $3 million (C$4.3 million) per tract in Thousand Oaks, Calif., according to the federal sale notice . “Plans for deep seabed mining must assess and prevent harm to endangered species, many of which are sacred to the Samoan people,” Elora Raymond, co-founder of community group Faʻasao Amerika Samoa, said in a statement released by Earthjustice on Tuesday. District Judge Jill Otake ruled Monday in Hawaii that the National Marine Fisheries Service violated the Endangered Species Act by considering only the effects of the sale and preliminary surveys on protected species. The agency also had to consider the exploration and mining that could follow, she ruled. The order set aside the fisheries agency’s approval letter.\nImpossible request\nThe leases cover metals in nodules and iron-manganese crusts on the seabed. A lease would allow preliminary work such as mapping. A successful bidder would need separate approvals and environmental reviews before more advanced exploration, testing or commercial mining, the administration said. The leasing process began with an April 2025 request from seabed developer Impossible Metals. Later that month, President Donald Trump directed federal agencies to speed up offshore mineral development to reduce reliance on foreign suppliers. American Samoa’s governor has asked Washington to share proceeds from bids, rents and royalties with the territory. The Marine Minerals Administration said it would need new legal authority to share those revenues. The agency has also proposed a sale near the Northern Mariana Islands and Guam. That proposal faces a similar challenge to the fisheries agency’s review, Otake noted in her ruling.\nPrint\nTAG: critical minerals deep sea mining environment\nCOMMODITY: Iron Ore manganese\nREGION: Asia United States\nRepublish this article",
